-- title:
--   Transport of a tangent invariant to dual-number points of R
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and write $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ for `Zp2 q`, the Witt vectors of the field $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ (`GaloisField q 2`).
--
--   **The base ring.** $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero and a $\mathbb{Z}_q$-algebra, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $q$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), and the residue field $k :=$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField Onr` is algebraically closed. A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is given, together with $X_0$, a special formal $O_D$-module over $k$ for the composite $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \xrightarrow{\iota} O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$: that is, a commutative two-dimensional formal group law over $k$ equipped with an action $a \mapsto \operatorname{act} a$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ by endomorphisms of the law, additive and multiplicative in $a$, and an endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = \operatorname{act}(q)$ and $\varpi \circ \operatorname{act}(a) = \operatorname{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt-vector Frobenius $\sigma$, which is special for the given embedding (the zero and one eigenspaces of the Lie algebra are complementary and both invertible as modules) and has height $4$ (the kernel of $\operatorname{act}(q)$ has degree $q^4$).
--
--   **The deformation datum.** $R$ is a commutative Noetherian local $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra, adically complete for its maximal ideal, equipped with a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ satisfying $\mathrm{res}_R \circ (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R) =$ the residue map of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (`hresR`); furthermore $X_u$ is a formal $O_D$-module over $R$ (a structure as above, without the speciality and height conditions) and $w_u$ is an isomorphism from the base change $X_u \otimes_{\mathrm{res}_R} k$ to $X_0$, isomorphism meaning a two-sided invertible morphism of formal $O_D$-modules (`hwu`).
--
--   **The pro-representability hypothesis `hPRO`.** For every Artinian local $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra $A$ and every surjective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_A : A \to k$ with $\mathrm{res}_A \circ (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to A)$ the residue map, for every formal $O_D$-module $X$ over $A$ which is special for $(O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to A) \circ \iota$ and has height $4$, and for every isomorphism $w$ from $X \otimes_{\mathrm{res}_A} k$ to $X_0$, there is a unique $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra homomorphism $\chi : R \to A$ such that $\mathrm{res}_A \circ \chi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and there is an isomorphism $v$ from $X_u \otimes_{\chi} A$ to $X$ whose reduction composed with $w$, namely $w \circ (v \otimes_{\mathrm{res}_A} k)$, has the same underlying power-series tuple as $w_u$.
--
--   **The tangent hypothesis `htangent`.** For a fixed $r \in \mathbb{N}$, there exists a function $e$ assigning to each formal $O_D$-module $X$ over the dual numbers $k[\varepsilon]$ (`DualNumber`) and each isomorphism $w$ from the reduction $X \otimes_{\mathrm{fst}} k$ to $X_0$, where $\mathrm{fst} : k[\varepsilon] \to k$ kills $\varepsilon$, a vector in $k^r$, subject to four clauses. (i) *Invariance*: if $v : X \to X'$ is an isomorphism over $k[\varepsilon]$ and the series of $w' \circ (v \otimes_{\mathrm{fst}} k)$ equals that of $w$, then $e(X,w) = e(X',w')$. (ii) *Injectivity on isomorphism classes*: if $e(X,w) = e(X',w')$ then such an isomorphism $v$ with $w' \circ (v \otimes_{\mathrm{fst}} k) = w$ exists. (iii) *Homogeneity*: for $c \in k$ and a ring endomorphism $\mu$ of $k[\varepsilon]$ with $\mathrm{fst} \circ \mu = \mathrm{fst}$ and $\mathrm{snd}(\mu t) = c\,\mathrm{snd}(t)$ for all $t$, and for any isomorphism $w'$ from $(X \otimes_\mu k[\varepsilon]) \otimes_{\mathrm{fst}} k$ to $X_0$ with the same series as $w$, one has $e(X \otimes_\mu k[\varepsilon], w') = c \cdot e(X,w)$. (iv) *Additivity*: for every commutative ring $B$ with a surjective local ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_B : B \to k$, for all ring homomorphisms $p_1, p_2, \sigma : B \to k[\varepsilon]$ lifting $\mathrm{res}_B$ (that is, $\mathrm{fst} \circ p_1 = \mathrm{fst} \circ p_2 = \mathrm{fst} \circ \sigma = \mathrm{res}_B$) and satisfying $\mathrm{snd}(\sigma b) = \mathrm{snd}(p_1 b) + \mathrm{snd}(p_2 b)$ for all $b$, for every formal $O_D$-module $Y$ over $B$ with an isomorphism $u$ from $Y \otimes_{\mathrm{res}_B} k$ to $X_0$, and for isomorphisms $w_1, w_2, w_\sigma$ from the reductions of $Y \otimes_{p_1} k[\varepsilon]$, $Y \otimes_{p_2} k[\varepsilon]$, $Y \otimes_{\sigma} k[\varepsilon]$ to $X_0$ whose series all equal that of $u$, one has $e(Y \otimes_\sigma k[\varepsilon], w_\sigma) = e(Y \otimes_{p_1} k[\varepsilon], w_1) + e(Y \otimes_{p_2} k[\varepsilon], w_2)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses there exists a function $e'$ from the set of all ring homomorphisms $R \to k[\varepsilon]$ to $k^r$ with the following three properties. Call $\chi : R \to k[\varepsilon]$ *admissible* if the two conditions $\mathrm{fst} \circ \chi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\chi \circ (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R) = (k \to k[\varepsilon]) \circ (\text{residue map of } O^{\mathrm{nr}})$ hold; both conditions are written out as a conjunction in each clause below.
--
--   First, $e'$ is injective on admissible homomorphisms: for admissible $\chi, \chi'$, $e'(\chi) = e'(\chi')$ implies $\chi = \chi'$. Secondly, $e'$ is homogeneous: for $c \in k$ and a ring endomorphism $\mu$ of $k[\varepsilon]$ with $\mathrm{fst} \circ \mu = \mathrm{fst}$ and $\mathrm{snd}(\mu t) = c\,\mathrm{snd}(t)$ for all $t$, and for every admissible $\chi$, one has $e'(\mu \circ \chi) = c \cdot e'(\chi)$. Thirdly, $e'$ is additive: for admissible $\chi_1, \chi_2, \chi_3$ with $\mathrm{snd}(\chi_3 x) = \mathrm{snd}(\chi_1 x) + \mathrm{snd}(\chi_2 x)$ for all $x \in R$, one has $e'(\chi_3) = e'(\chi_1) + e'(\chi_2)$. No formula for $e'$ and no property of $e'$ at non-admissible homomorphisms is asserted.
--
--   This is the Schlessinger-style comparison between the tangent space of the deformation functor of a special formal $O_D$-module and the dual-number points of a ring pro-representing it: a numerical invariant of first-order deformations, injective on isomorphism classes and linear, is carried over to the $k[\varepsilon]$-points of $R$. It is used, together with the identification of special formal $O_D$-modules over Artinian local rings of the relevant type, in bounding the maximal ideal of $R$ and thereby in determining the local structure of the Čerednik–Drinfeld deformation spaces.
